{"title":"1969 IMO Problems","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Problems of the 11th <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"IMO\">IMO</a> 1969 in Romania. \n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Problem_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Problem_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Problem_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Problem_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Problem_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 6</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_1\">Problem 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Prove that there are infinitely many natural numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> with the following property: the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/3/8a3744d2b20b3e44f8d1204739ebdca4464cf411.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z = n^4 + a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"83\" height=\"16\" /> is not prime for any natural number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_2\">Problem 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/4/e047bc14560bc613b17fb1ba735bba221764510a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, \\cdots, a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"103\" height=\"11\" /> be real constants, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> a real variable, and\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/2/3425473bdd88011618e4f9f3f58c50609fa5bb1d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(x) = \\cos(a_1 + x) + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos(a_2 + x) + \\frac{1}{4}\\cos(a_3 + x) + \\cdots + \\frac{1}{2^{n - 1}}\\cos(a_n + x).\\]\" width=\"536\" height=\"37\" />\nGiven that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/a/3aa244ceb807ee6d2574bed3abd13754d8283911.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x_1) = f(x_2) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"142\" height=\"18\" />, prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/6/39698c11e14ef47fe31dfa1316f59791961a5f9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_2 - x_1 = m\\pi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"108\" height=\"10\" /> for some integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_3\">Problem 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For each value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/7/8d7f1245212fe388ab0f6309d24d350604ee5f2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"111\" height=\"16\" />, find necessary and sufficient conditions on the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/5/d25634d7212302efdeb3702974863759bfb74d6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"45\" height=\"15\" /> so that there exists a tetrahedron with k edges of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, and the remaining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/a/99a5852376629551369d8ab20e97544cc78c84c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 - k$\" width=\"41\" height=\"12\" /> edges of length 1.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_4\">Problem 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A semicircular arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/9/66981fa3920210c6ad8dbe5e968783d5dd7520c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" /> is drawn on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> as diameter. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a point on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/9/66981fa3920210c6ad8dbe5e968783d5dd7520c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" /> other than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is the foot of the perpendicular from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. We consider three circles, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/1/991ebbe6f7e1ca92a396a8aa5378f69e01f778db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma_1, \\gamma_2, \\gamma_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"65\" height=\"11\" />, all tangent to the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. Of these, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/7/24746dfd17e9188871007307efc793c9be692647.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"11\" /> is inscribed in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6d5d23a7e91fb9f6c248fb4cd43111165540f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta ABC$\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" />, while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/0/d80204ef985e41e2df36b4bc75c2c93952c4bee4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/d/47d6360d88517c8226fe5e3157eb403e6334492e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"11\" /> are both tangent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> and to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/9/66981fa3920210c6ad8dbe5e968783d5dd7520c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" />, one on each side of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />. Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/7/24746dfd17e9188871007307efc793c9be692647.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"11\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/0/d80204ef985e41e2df36b4bc75c2c93952c4bee4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/d/47d6360d88517c8226fe5e3157eb403e6334492e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"11\" /> have a second tangent in common.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_5\">Problem 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/6/e460097db4d7cd8b841ec11a5c9de2fd8ecde92b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n &gt; 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> points in the plane such that no three are collinear. Prove that there are at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/1/2118e2cdab558bab183e94f300c36ec268c5d088.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tbinom{n - 3}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"39\" height=\"22\" /> convex quadrilaterals whose vertices are four of the given points.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_6\">Problem 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Prove that for all real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/1/a71bbde9ff55f7d4098d3492165a9a792d778ab8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1, x_2, y_1, y_2, z_1, z_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"142\" height=\"13\" />, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/9/a8993a62ac971460eb0316ca87017795433817a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 &gt; 0, x_2 &gt; 0, x_1y_1 - z_1^2 &gt; 0, x_2y_2 - z_2^2 &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"341\" height=\"21\" />, the inequality\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/6/d064cd5e072e62dbbd229299796392c38859cd85.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{8}{(x_1 + x_2)(y_1 + y_2) - (z_1 + z_2)^2} \\leq \\frac{1}{x_1y_1 - z_1^2} + \\frac{1}{x_2y_2 - z_2^2}\\]\" width=\"440\" height=\"42\" />\nis satisfied. Give necessary and sufficient conditions for equality.\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO\" title=\"1969 IMO\">1969 IMO</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=1&amp;cid=16&amp;year=1969\">IMO 1969 Problems on the Resources page</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions,_with_authors\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors\">IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematics_competition_resources\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Mathematics competition resources\">Mathematics competition resources</a></li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO\" title=\"1969 IMO\">1969 IMO</a></b> (<b><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1968_IMO\" title=\"1968 IMO\">1968 IMO</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1969 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1970_IMO\" title=\"1970 IMO\">1970 IMO</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018042000\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.028 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.044 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 234/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 837/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 56/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 7107/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    8.226      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    8.226      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wik